// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  WHY THIS BOOKLET
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#fit-page("why")[
  #band("Why this booklet")

  #lead("Every year, cities buy software: parking systems, sensor platforms, map portals, digital twins. Too often, the data those systems collect ends up locked inside them. When the contract ends, the data — paid for with public money — leaves with the supplier, or stays trapped in a format nobody else can read.")

  #lead("It does not have to be this way. The Minimal Interoperability Mechanisms (MIMs), maintained by Open & Agile Smart Cities (OASC) and endorsed through the European living-in.eu movement, describe the minimal technical requirements that keep your data open, reusable and vendor-neutral. They are deliberately small: not a grand architecture, just the few things that must be true so that systems can talk to each other.")

  #lead("You do not need to be an engineer to use them. Each checklist turns one MIM into plain-words checklist items you can copy into a tender, with the reason why each one protects you. Right after the checklist you find every requirement of that MIM, word for word, so your technical colleagues and your bidders can check the details.")

  #lead("Cities that require the MIMs buy once and reuse many times: the air-quality data feeds the school dashboard, the streetlight inventory feeds the energy plan, and the next supplier picks up where the last one left off. That is what interoperability means in practice — and it is increasingly expected in EU-funded projects.")

  #pull-quote(
    "The minimal interoperability mechanisms (MIMs) enable a minimal but sufficient level of interoperability for data, systems, and services specifically in the context of smart city solutions. · The intended audience for this document are: · 1. Solution providers that wish to make sure their solutions are interoperable with other and allow fluid exchange of data within a Smart Cities and Communities context. · 2. Procurers that wish to procure solutions that are future-proof and allow cost-effective integration within an existing Smart Cities and Communities Information and Communication Technology (ICT) architecture.",
  )

  #policy-box("How the MIMs fit EU law",
    "The MIMs are a technical specification, not a law. They point in the same direction as EU rules that already apply to public data and digital services:",
    law("Public Procurement Directive", "Directive 2014/24/EU, Article 42",
      "technical specifications may refer to standards, always with “or equivalent”; as a rule they may not name a specific make or product."),
    law("Data Act", "Regulation (EU) 2023/2854",
      "access to data from connected products, and switching between cloud providers without lock-in."),
    law("Interoperable Europe Act", "Regulation (EU) 2024/903",
      "interoperability assessments for cross-border digital public services, and reuse of open solutions."),
    law("Open Data Directive", "Directive (EU) 2019/1024",
      "high-value datasets published free of charge, machine-readable, through APIs."),
    law("NIS2 Directive", "Directive (EU) 2022/2555",
      "a cybersecurity baseline; national law decides how far it covers local administration."),
    law("GDPR", "Regulation (EU) 2016/679",
      "personal data in city systems (parking, cameras, mobility)."),
    law("AI Act", "Regulation (EU) 2024/1689",
      "rules for artificial intelligence systems; relevant for AI models in digital twins."),
    note: "For orientation only. Check how your country has transposed each act.",
  )
]
#pagebreak()


// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  HOW TO USE THIS BOOKLET
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#fit-page("howto")[
  #band("How to use this booklet")

  #steps(
    step("Prepare",
      "Read the seven checklists. Mark the ones that apply to what you are buying. Most tenders need MIM0, MIM1, MIM2 and MIM6; add MIM3 when data is shared with third parties or through a data portal, MIM7 for anything with a map, MIM8 for digital twins."),
    step("Tender",
      "Copy the ticked checklist items into your requirements. Each item is written as a sentence you can paste. Add the cross-cutting clauses: they cost little and prevent lock-in.",
      links: (("clauses", "Clauses"), ("example", "Example"), )),
    step("Evaluate",
      "Ask bidders to show, not promise (see the clause of that name). Verify compliance against the compliance table the bidder completed.",
      links: (("table", "Compliance table"), )),
    step("Deliver",
      "At acceptance, re-test every item you ticked. A requirement that is not tested at delivery was never really a requirement.",
      links: (("table", "Compliance table"), )),
  )

  #legend("Reading the specification quotes",
    note: "The checklist items say “must” even where the specification only recommends (should) or lists a candidate mechanism. That is the contracting authority’s decision.",
    entry("SHALL / MUST", "mandatory. The system does not comply without it."),
    entry("MUST NOT", "forbidden. A system that does it does not comply."),
    entry("SHOULD", "strongly recommended. Deviations need a good written reason."),
    entry("MAY", "optional. Nice to have, never required."),
    entry("CAN", "a possibility, not a requirement. It says what is possible and obliges nobody."),
  )

  #note("Every checklist item carries a small reference like → MIM0 R1.1. That is the requirement in MIMs Plus 9.0 it comes from; right after the checklist you find every requirement of that MIM word for word. Only MIM0 defines the keywords: they count when written in capitals, the convention of IETF RFC 2119 and RFC 8174 (CAN comes from the ISO/IEC Directives, Part 2). The other MIMs have no definition: MIM6 writes SHALL mostly in capitals, MIM2, MIM3, MIM7 and MIM8 write shall, should and must in lower case, and MIM1 uses them only in the description of a mechanism (MUST NOT in section 3.1). This booklet reads the lower-case words the same way; a bidder may read them more loosely.")
]
#pagebreak()


